Presidential elections were held in [[Mongolia]] on 6 June 1993,<ref>[[Dieter Nohlen]], Florian Grotz & Christof Hartmann (2001) ''Elections in Asia: A data handbook, Volume II'', p490 {{ISBN|0-19-924959-8}}</ref> the first time a [[President of Mongolia|head of state]] had been democratically elected in a competitive election. The two candidates were incumbent [[president of Mongolia|president]] [[Punsalmaagiin Ochirbat]] and [[Лодонгийн Түдэв|Lodongiin Tüdev]], a renowned writer, editor and prominent political figure.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Renowned Mongolian writer Lodongiin Tudev passes away |url=https://www.montsame.mn/en/read/222244 |access-date=2024-10-05 |website=MONTSAME News Agency |language=en}}</ref> Ochirbat, who was previously elected [[President of the Mongolian People's Republic|president]] of the [[Mongolian People's Republic]] by the [[People's Great Khural|People's Grand Khural]] in the aftermath of the [[Mongolian Revolution of 1990|1990 revolution]], was nominated as a candidate by the opposition coalition between the [[Mongolian Social Democratic Party]] (MSDP) and the [[Mongolian National Democratic Party (1992–1999)|Mongolian National Democratic Party]] (MNDP). Tüdev was nominated as a candidate by the ruling [[Mongolian People's Party|Mongolian People's Revolutionary Party]] (MPRP).

The result was a victory for Ochirbat, who received 57.8% of the vote. Voter turnout was 93%,<ref>Nohlen ''et al''., p491</ref><ref>{{Cite web |date=2023 |title=Elections in Mongolia (1992-2021) |url=https://asiafoundation.org/insight/elections-in-mongolia-1992-2021/ |access-date=2026-10-03 |website=The Asia Foundation |page=85 |language=en-US}}</ref> which remains the highest turnout for a presidential election and the only time the turnout for a presidential election was over 90%. It was the MPRP's first electoral loss since the adoption of the [[Constitution of Mongolia|1992 Constitution]].<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Batbayar |first=Tsedendambyn |date=1994 |title=Mongolia in 1993: A Fragile Democracy |url=https://www.jstor.org/stable/2644955 |journal=Asian Survey |volume=34 |issue=1 |pages=41–45 |doi=10.2307/2644955 |issn=0004-4687|url-access=subscription }}</ref>

== Electoral system ==
Presidential electoral law was ratified by the legislature on 15 February 1993, which took effect on 1 March 1993.<ref name=":4">{{Cite web |date=1993-02-15 |title=МОНГОЛ УЛСЫН ЕРӨНХИЙЛӨГЧИЙН СОНГУУЛИЙН ХУУЛЬ |url=https://legalinfo.mn/ |access-date=2026-10-03 |website=Эрх зүйн мэдээллийн нэгдсэн систем |language=en}}</ref>

According to the law, the election was to be held using the [[two-round system]]. If no candidate had received a majority of the vote (including invalid ballots) in the first round, a second round would be held. Only parties with seats in the [[State Great Khural]] were allowed to nominate candidates. Parties in a coalition were also allowed to nominate a joint candidate.<ref name=":4" />

Citizens aged at least 18 and residing within the borders of Mongolia were eligible to vote via [[secret ballot]]. The organization of the elections were tasked to [[General Election Commission of Mongolia|General Election Commission]] (GEC), an electoral commission responsible to the State Great Khural, and local polling stations.<ref name=":4" />

==Background==
===Fall of communism===
{{Main|1990 Democratic Revolution in Mongolia}}
Following the [[Mongolian Revolution of 1990|1990 pro-democracy movement]], the [[General Secretary of the Mongolian People's Revolutionary Party]], [[Jambyn Batmönkh]], and other [[politburo]] leaders announced their resignation in March 1990, paving the way for democratic change in Mongolia.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web |last=Б. |date=2015-07-29 |title=МОНГОЛД АРДЧИЛАЛ ЭХЭЛСЭН ТҮҮХ |url=https://ikon.mn/p/irg |access-date=2024-10-05 |website=ikon.mn |language=mn|first=Дөлгөөн}}</ref><ref name=":3">{{cite journal |last1=Heaton |first1=William R. |date=Jan 1991 |title=Mongolia in 1990: Upheaval, Reform, But No Revolution Yet |journal=The Western Political Quarterly |volume=31 |issue=1 |pages=50–56 |doi=10.2307/2645184 |jstor=2645184}}</ref>

On 21 March 1990, during the 8th Extraordinary [[People's Great Khural|People's Grand Khural]] session, the following constitutional provision: "The guiding force of the [[Mongolian People's Republic]] is the Mongolian People's Revolutionary Party, which uses the all-conquering [[Marxism–Leninism|Marxist-Leninist theory]] in its operations." was removed from the [[Constitutions of the Mongolian People's Republic|Constitution of the MPR]]; officially ending 70 years of [[One-party state|one-party rule]].<ref name=":1" /> Head of state Jambyn Batmönkh and premier [[Dumaagiin Sodnom]] were succeeded by [[Punsalmaagiin Ochirbat]] and [[Sharavyn Gungaadorj]] during the session.<ref name=":3" />

===Transition to democracy===
The [[1990 Mongolian parliamentary election|first multi-party parliamentary elections]] were held in July 1990 for the People's Grand Khural and the [[Little Khural|State Little Khural]] with a high turnout of 98%. The MPRP won both elections to both houses, while opposition parties made modest gains. Punsalmaagiin Ochirbat was re-elected as a deputy to the People's Grand Khural and was subsequently appointed to the newly created position of [[President of the Mongolian People's Republic]] in September 1990.<ref>{{Cite web |last=[[Baabar|Баабар]] |first= |title=Монголын ардчилсан хувьсгалын хроник |url=http://www.baabar.mn/article/1070 |access-date=2026-01-28 |website=www.baabar.mn |language=mn}}</ref> 
[[File:People's Grand Khural 1990.jpg|left|thumb|President [[Punsalmaagiin Ochirbat]] (leftmost) and deputy premier [[Dashiin Byambasüren]] (rightmost) meeting with opposition leaders, circa June 1990]]
In January 1992, a [[Constitution of Mongolia|new constitution]] was ratified, renaming the country to [[Mongolia]] and changing the presidential title to "[[President of Mongolia|President of Mongolia and Commander-in-Chief of the Armed Forces]]."<ref>{{Cite web |last=А. |first=Төгөлдөр |date=2025-01-13 |title=1992 оноос хойш дөрвөн удаа Үндсэн хуульд өөрчлөлт оруулжээ |url=https://ikon.mn/n/3bof |access-date=2026-01-28 |website=ikon.mn |language=mn}}</ref> After the ratification, [[1992 Mongolian parliamentary election|parliamentary elections]] for the unicameral legislature, the [[State Great Khural]], were held on 28 June 1992. The MPRP once again won a [[supermajority]], securing 70 out of the total 76 seats with 57% of the vote.<ref>{{Cite web |last=З. |first=Санжаа |date=2024-06-19 |title=ТАНИЛЦ: 1992-2020 он хүртэл УИХ-ын сонгуулийн тойрог хуваалт ба үр дүн |url=https://ikon.mn/n/1ra9 |access-date=2026-01-28 |website=ikon.mn |language=mn}}</ref> This was largely attributed to the party's adoption of [[Multi-party system|multiparty governance]] and its support for a [[market economy]]. The opposition alliance between the [[Mongolian National Progress Party]] (MNPP), the [[Mongolian Democratic Party (1990)|Mongolian Democratic Party]] (MDP), and the [[Mongolian United Party]] (MUP) won four seats with 17% of the vote. The [[Mongolian Social Democratic Party]], supported by the [[Social Democratic Party of Germany|German SPD]], won one seat with 10% of the vote.<ref name=":0" /> The opposition MDP, MNPP, MUP, and [[Mongolian Renaissance Party]] merged in late 1992 to form the [[Mongolian National Democratic Party (1992–2000)|Mongolian National Democratic Party]] (MNDP). The MNDP, with its four parliamentary seats, became the primary opposition to the MPRP and second largest parliamentary party.

Local elections were held in October of the same year. The ruling MPRP won all 18 governorships of the country's [[Aimag|provinces]] and almost 90% of all [[Sum (administrative division)|sum]] legislature seats.<ref name=":0">{{Cite web |title=Report on Mongolian Presidential Election: June 6, 1993 |url=https://www.ifes.org/publications/report-mongolian-presidential-election-june-6-1993 |access-date=2024-10-05 |website=[[IFES]] |language=en|pages=3-4}}</ref>

==Candidates==
According to the electoral law, parties with representation in the State Great Khural were allowed to nominate candidates for the presidential election.<ref name=":4" /> Three political parties, the MPRP, the MNDP, and the MSDP, were eligible to compete in the election.

During a party conference in April 1993, inaugural president Ochirbat was rejected as the MPRP's pick for the presidential candidate, with the party instead selecting [[Lodongiin Tüdev]], a well-known writer, editor, and intellectual figure. Both figures belonged to different factions within the party.<ref name=":0" /> Tüdev was considered as more orthodox party member than Ochirbat.<ref>{{Cite book |title=Nations in transit, 1999-2000: civil society, democracy, and markets in East Central Europe and the newly independent states |date=2001 |publisher=Transaction Publishers |isbn=978-0-7658-0773-1 |editor-last=Karatnycky |editor-first=Adrian |location=New Brunswick, [N.J.] |pages=465-466 |editor-last2=Motyl |editor-first2=Alexander |editor-last3=Piano |editor-first3=Aili}}</ref>

In response to the decision, the MNDP and MSDP formed a coalition and nominated Ochirbat as their joint candidate for the presidential election.<ref name=":0" />
